# ============================ SYSADMIN 1 ============================
LESSONS_S1 = [
L("Chapter 1 — The sysadmin function on a managed platform", "11", "<p>This track certifies the <b>system administrator</b>: the person who owns the ZhiftERP machinery itself — its users and permissions, its master data governance, its backups, its changes, its channels, and its health. Every other track teaches operating a module; this one teaches operating the platform they all run on.</p><p><b>The division of custody.</b> ZhiftERP on CloudERP.One is a managed platform, and the custody line must be learned on day one:</p><p>• <b>The host (CloudERP.One)</b> owns the infrastructure layer: the servers, the database engine, the platform updates, the off-site backup machinery, uptime, and the network. Problems at that layer are <b>raised</b>, not fixed locally — Chapter 8 covers the support channel and what a good report contains.<br>• <b>The site sysadmin (this certification)</b> owns everything inside the site: users, roles and permissions; System Settings and the module settings; master data governance; the backup verification habit; change control for customisations; email, printing, and integrations; and the monitoring reads.</p><p>The sysadmin owns the <b>machinery, not the data</b>: accounts policy belongs to the accountant, stock policy to the stock owner. The sysadmin makes their policies executable — a role built, a validation enabled, a setting recorded — and keeps the platform trustworthy underneath them.</p><p><b>Why the function is a named role.</b> Systems without a named sysadmin all drift the same way: everyone is a System Manager because setup was easier that way; settings change per complaint and nobody knows when; masters duplicate; leavers keep logins; the backup has never been test-restored; and the first serious incident finds no owner. Every one of those is a module of this track, and the fix in every case begins the same way: <b>one named person owns the machinery</b>, with a named backstop.</p><p><b>What the role is not.</b> Not a developer (customisations are governed in module 5, not written here), not the data's owner, and not a bottleneck — the whole design goal is a platform so well-governed that daily work never waits for the sysadmin.</p><p><b>Module contents.</b> Chapter 2: the platform's anatomy — sites, apps, and the Desk. Chapter 3: DocTypes and documents — the system's grammar. Chapter 4: lists, filters, and reports. Chapter 5: the System Settings walk. Chapter 6: workspaces and the module map. Chapter 7: the sysadmin calendar. Chapter 8: the support channel and the HealthTrade case. Chapter 9: review and the law.</p>"),
L("Chapter 2 — The platform's anatomy: site, apps, and the Desk", "12", "<p>Four terms carry the whole architecture. Learn them precisely — every support conversation and every module of this track uses them.</p><p><b>The site.</b> The business's own installation: its database, its files, its users, its settings. One site is one business system, isolated from every other site on the platform. Everything this track governs lives inside the site.</p><p><b>The apps.</b> The software the site runs: the framework (the engine — forms, permissions, reports, email), the ERP application (the business doctypes — Sales Invoice, Item, Journal Entry), and any extension apps the implementation added. The installed list is visible from <b>Help &gt; About</b> — the versions shown there are the first thing the host's support asks for. Apps are code and belong to the host's update stream (module 5); the site's <b>data and settings</b> are the sysadmin's.</p><p><b>The Desk.</b> The working interface at <b>/app</b> — workspaces on the left, the <b>awesome bar</b> (the search box at the top) as the fastest route to anything. Learn the awesome bar's three uses now, because this track's instructions lean on it:</p><p>1. Type a doctype's name to jump to its list — <i>Sales Invoice</i> opens the invoice list.<br>2. Type <i>new</i> plus a doctype to create — <i>new user</i> opens a blank User.<br>3. Type a settings or tool name to open it directly — <i>System Settings</i>, <i>Role Permission Manager</i>, <i>Data Import</i>.</p><p>The <b>portal</b> is the other face: the website side where customers, suppliers, or — in this platform's case — Duty Board rooms are served to non-Desk users. Chapter 2 of module 2 makes the Desk/portal distinction precise at the user level, because it decides both licensing and exposure.</p><p><b>The bench.</b> The host-side machinery that runs sites: process management, scheduled jobs, updates, server backups. On the managed platform the bench is CloudERP.One's custody. The sysadmin needs the word because support conversations use it — <i>the bench was updated</i>, <i>a bench job failed</i> — and because module 4 and 5 describe what the host does there on the site's behalf.</p><p><b>The custody map, restated on the anatomy.</b> Site data and settings: the sysadmin. App code and versions: the host's stream, received governed (module 5). The bench and below: the host, reached through the support channel with the About-page versions in hand.</p><p>Practical rule: when anything is reported broken, place it on this map first. \"A user cannot see a report\" is site custody (permissions — module 2). \"The site is down\" is bench custody (raise it, with the time and what changed). The map decides who moves.</p>"),
L("Chapter 3 — DocTypes and documents: the system's grammar", "12", "<p>Everything in ZhiftERP is a <b>DocType</b> — a form definition — and every record is a <b>document</b> of one. The sysadmin reads this grammar fluently, because permissions, imports, reports, and customisations all speak it.</p><p><b>The anatomy of a DocType.</b> Open any document and read it as structure:</p><p>• <b>Fields</b> — each with a type (Data, Link, Select, Date, Currency, Table…). The type decides behaviour: a <b>Link field</b> points at another doctype's records (the Customer field on a Sales Invoice links to the Customer doctype), which is how the system stays consistent — you cannot invoice a customer who does not exist.<br>• <b>Child tables</b> — the rows inside a document (the Items table on an invoice) are themselves documents of a child doctype.<br>• <b>Naming</b> — every document has a unique name/ID, produced by its naming rule: a series (SINV-2026-00042), the field's own value (a Customer named by customer name), or a set format. Module 3 governs series properly.<br>• <b>Mandatory and read-only</b> markings, defaults, and validations — the form refusing a bad save is the permission layer's cousin: policy enforced at entry.</p><p><b>The document lifecycle — docstatus.</b> Transaction doctypes carry three states, and the whole audit design of the system hangs on them:</p><p>• <b>Draft (0)</b> — editable, posts nothing. Draft sweeps at close (the Accounts track's cut-off) exist because drafts are invisible to the books.<br>• <b>Submitted (1)</b> — posted and locked. Submission is when a Sales Invoice writes its GL rows and stock moves. Submitted documents are not edited — the framework refuses it.<br>• <b>Cancelled (2)</b> — reversed, retained. Cancel posts the reversal and keeps the record; <b>Amend</b> creates the corrected successor linked to it. Nothing honest is ever deleted.</p><p>Master doctypes (Customer, Item, User) have no docstatus — they are current-state records, edited in place, with the audit trail carrying their history (module 8).</p><p><b>Standard versus custom.</b> Standard doctypes and fields ship with the apps; <b>Custom Fields</b> and customisations are the site's own additions. The distinction matters twice: updates preserve customisations but standard structures may evolve under them (module 5's test discipline), and every custom field is a governance event — named, documented, owned — not a casual addition (module 5).</p><p>Practical rule: when a form behaves unexpectedly, read it as grammar before suspecting a fault — is the field a Link whose target is missing, a mandatory unfilled, a docstatus refusing edits? Most \"bugs\" reported to a sysadmin are the grammar working exactly as designed.</p>"),
L("Chapter 4 — Lists, filters, and the report machinery", "12", "<p>The sysadmin answers most questions with a list view or a report, and teaches the business to answer its own. This chapter is that machinery.</p><p><b>List views.</b> Every doctype's list (awesome bar → the doctype's name) carries the working toolkit:</p><p>• <b>Filters</b> — click Filter, or use the quick filters in the sidebar: Status = Draft, dated this month, by company, by owner. Filters chain — this is the close's draft sweep, the leaver's document check, the monthly journal review, all built from the same control.<br>• <b>Sort, and the count.</b> The list's count is itself a read — module 2's user reviews and module 7's health reads are lists counted.<br>• <b>Saved filters</b> — a filter set worth repeating is saved and named; it appears in the sidebar for one click next month. Routine reads (drafts this month, disabled users, failed emails) should all be one click by the end of this track.<br>• <b>Bulk actions</b> — select rows for bulk edit, assignment, or export. Powerful and therefore governed: bulk edit rights follow the same permission design as everything else (module 2).<br>• <b>The report view</b> — the list flipped into columns, with column choice, grouping, and totals. For most operational questions the report view of the right list <b>is</b> the report.</p><p><b>The three report kinds.</b></p><p>1. <b>Report Builder reports</b> — saved report views: columns, filters, sort, group-bys, built without code and shared by name. The sysadmin's default answer to \"can we get a report of…\" — built in minutes, owned by the asker.<br>2. <b>Standard reports</b> — the shipped analytical reports each module's track leans on (General Ledger, Stock Ledger, Accounts Receivable…). Script-driven, filter-rich, and drillable.<br>3. <b>Custom query/script reports</b> — code, and therefore module 5's territory: specified, tested, documented, owned. A business that jumps to custom reports before exhausting the first two kinds is buying maintenance it does not need.</p><p><b>Exports.</b> Any list or report exports to spreadsheet. Two governance notes ride along: export permission is a real permission (module 2's matrix includes it — customer lists walk out of businesses as exports), and an exported spreadsheet is a <b>snapshot that starts aging immediately</b> — the standing answer to spreadsheet-first habits is the saved filter or report that reads live.</p><p>Practical rule: before building anything, ask which existing list answers the question. The sysadmin's craft is mostly knowing that the report already exists.</p>"),
L("Chapter 5 — The System Settings walk", "13", "<p>Open <b>System Settings</b> (awesome bar). This is the site-wide policy page, and the sysadmin walks it exactly as the Accounts track walks Accounts Settings: each field read as the policy question it is, the answers recorded in the implementation file, changes after go-live as governed events, and an annual re-walk on the calendar.</p><p><b>The identity block.</b> Country, time zone, date format, number format, first day of the week, and the float/currency <b>precision</b>. Set once at birth, correctly: the time zone stamps every document and log; the formats print on every invoice; precision inconsistencies produce permanent kobo-level reconciliation noise (the Accounts track meets this from the other side). Changing these mid-life is a governed event with a reason, not a preference toggle.</p><p><b>The session and login block.</b> <b>Session Expiry</b> (idle sessions die — hours, not weeks, on a system holding the business's books), <b>Allow Consecutive Login Attempts</b> and the lockout window (brute force answered by the platform, not by hope), <b>Allow Login Using Mobile Number / User Name</b> (decide the identity story deliberately — email is the clean default), and the <b>Forgot Password</b> flow enabled so resets are self-service and logged instead of whispered to an admin.</p><p><b>The password block.</b> Minimum <b>Password Strength / Score</b> enforced by the platform — the policy that works is the one users cannot decline. Module 8 completes this with two-factor authentication.</p><p><b>The files block.</b> Maximum attachment size, and allowed file types where the platform exposes them. Attachments are evidence (every track attaches documents to entries), so the setting balances evidence habits against storage — and storage is a monitored number in module 7.</p><p><b>The backups line.</b> The scheduled backup count kept on-site (module 4 owns the whole subject; the setting is noted on this walk so it is never a surprise).</p><p><b>The updates and notifications block.</b> How the site learns of platform changes, and the email defaults that module 6 completes.</p><p><b>The walk's three rules — identical to every settings walk in the programme:</b></p><p>1. Every field is a policy question; the answer is written down with its reason.<br>2. Post-go-live changes are governed events — reason, record, and where user-facing, an announcement.<br>3. The annual walk is the routine occasion for change; complaint-driven toggling is how sites drift into states nobody can explain.</p><p>Practical rule: the recorded walk is the difference between <i>settings</i> and <i>decisions</i>. A site whose System Settings page can be read aloud with a reason per field passes this module's core test.</p>"),
L("Chapter 6 — Workspaces and the module map", "12", "<p>The Desk's left sidebar is <b>workspaces</b> — the doors people walk through every day. The sysadmin curates them, because an uncurated Desk taxes every user every day.</p><p><b>What a workspace is.</b> A configurable page of shortcuts, report links, and cards for one working domain: Accounting, Stock, Selling, HR. Users see the workspaces their roles and module access allow (module 2 wires this); within that, workspaces can be edited — shortcuts added, cards arranged — and custom workspaces created for job families.</p><p><b>Curation, as a procedure.</b></p><p>1. <b>Per job family, list the daily objects:</b> the cashier's four doctypes, the storekeeper's six, the accountant's dozen. The lists are short — that is the point.<br>2. <b>Build or trim the workspace to the list:</b> the daily doctypes and their New buttons as shortcuts, the role's two or three standing reports (Chapter 4's saved filters and Report Builder reports) linked by name, everything else removed from sight.<br>3. <b>Hide the modules the role never uses</b> — module 2's allowed-modules control per user — so the sidebar shows a person their own system, not the whole platform.<br>4. <b>Review at the annual walk:</b> shortcuts nobody clicks are removed; the new routine report earns its link.</p><p><b>Why this is a sysadmin duty and not decoration.</b> Three concrete returns:</p><p>• <b>Training cost.</b> A new cashier facing four shortcuts learns in a day; one facing forty doctypes learns fear. Every onboarding in every track lands on the Desk the sysadmin curated.<br>• <b>Error rate.</b> People misfile into doctypes they can see but should not touch. The workspace that shows only the job's objects removes a whole error class before permissions even engage.<br>• <b>Adoption.</b> The system the owner reads from daily is the one whose workspace answers the owner's three questions on one screen — the statements, the cash position, the exceptions. Module 7 builds that read; the workspace is where it lives.</p><p><b>The module map.</b> Alongside curation, the sysadmin keeps the one-page map of which modules the business actually runs — Accounts, Stock, Selling, Buying, HR, and the platform's own Duty Board — with each module's owner named. The map drives the allowed-modules defaults, the workspace set, and module 5's impact analysis (a change to Stock touches these people, this workspace, these reports).</p><p>Practical rule: audit the Desk from a new employee's seat once a year — log in as a test user of each job family and count the clicks to their daily work. More than two clicks to a daily object is a curation fault.</p>"),
L("Chapter 7 — The sysadmin calendar", "12", "<p>The function runs on a calendar, like every governed function in this programme. Each line has a named owner and a written procedure; most lines are single-digit minutes once the routines settle.</p><p><b>Daily — the five-minute read (module 7 details each):</b></p><p>• The <b>Error Log</b> scanned — new error types investigated, known noise acknowledged.<br>• The <b>email queue</b> glanced — stuck outbound mail is business documents not arriving (module 6).<br>• The <b>scheduled jobs</b> confirmed running — a dead scheduler silently stops backups, emails, and auto-repeats.<br>• Yesterday's <b>backup</b> confirmed present (module 4's daily line).</p><p><b>Weekly:</b></p><p>• The <b>user delta</b>: new users, disabled users, role changes this week — read from the User list and reconciled against HR's joiner/leaver notes (module 2's lifecycle).<br>• The <b>failed logins / lockouts</b> read for patterns.<br>• <b>Storage and performance</b> numbers noted — trends, not points (module 7).</p><p><b>Monthly:</b></p><p>• The <b>restore verification</b> — module 4's law: a backup untested is a hope. One backup, restored to the test site, opened and read.<br>• The <b>permission delta review</b> — what changed in the Role Permission Manager this month, by whom, why (module 2).<br>• The <b>master hygiene read</b> — duplicate candidates, naming drift, orphaned records (module 3).<br>• The <b>integration health check</b> — every connected channel exercised or read (module 6).</p><p><b>Quarterly:</b></p><p>• The <b>access recertification</b> — every active user re-confirmed by their manager: right roles, right restrictions, still employed (module 2's strongest control).<br>• The <b>update review</b> — the host's release notes read against the site; the test-then-live cycle run for pending changes (module 5).<br>• The <b>incident log</b> read by cause — repeat causes are process faults.</p><p><b>Annually:</b></p><p>• The <b>System Settings walk</b> (Chapter 5) and the module settings walks with their owners.<br>• The <b>security review</b> — 2FA coverage, password policy, API keys and integration credentials rotated (module 8).<br>• The <b>full continuity rehearsal</b> — the restore drill at full dress (module 4).<br>• The <b>workspace and Desk audit</b> (Chapter 6) and the <b>customisation register</b> reconciled (module 5).</p><p>Practical rule: the calendar is the function. A sysadmin without one is a firefighter, and the fires pick the schedule — install the calendar in month one and let module 7's reads populate it.</p>"),
L("Chapter 8 — The support channel & the HealthTrade case", "13", "<p><b>The support channel.</b> Custody-map problems that belong to the host are raised through the CloudERP.One support channel — and the quality of the report decides the speed of the fix. A good report carries five things:</p><p>1. <b>The site and the versions</b> — from Help &gt; About.<br>2. <b>The exact time</b> the problem started, with the time zone.<br>3. <b>What changed</b> — an update received, a setting changed, an import run, nothing known.<br>4. <b>The reproduction</b> — the steps that show it, the user it affects, the error text <b>copied whole</b> (and the matching Error Log entry — module 7 teaches reading it).<br>5. <b>The scope</b> — one user or all, one doctype or everywhere, constant or intermittent.</p><p>The sysadmin triages before raising: is it the custody map's site side (a permission, a setting, a filter) — fixed locally; or the platform side — raised with the five items. Both outcomes are logged in the incident book, because module 7's quarterly read mines that log for repeat causes.</p><p><b>The HealthTrade case — the function installed.</b> HealthTrade arrives at this track as the other tracks left it: modules well-operated, and the platform beneath them owned by nobody — the implementation partner had been the accidental sysadmin, and the gap shows the standard symptoms: five System Managers, settings nobody can explain, an untested backup, and a Desk showing every user everything.</p><p><b>Month one — the function is named.</b> The operations lead takes the role with the accountant as backstop; both are written into the policy page. The custody map is drawn on one page and taught in one meeting: what the business owns, what CloudERP.One owns, and the five-item report format for the boundary.</p><p><b>Month two — the ground is read.</b> The About page is recorded. The System Settings walk runs field by field with answers written — session expiry tightened from the install default, the password score enforced, precision confirmed against the accounts setup. The module map is drawn with owners, and the first Desk audit runs from a test cashier login: <b>eleven clicks</b> to reach the till's daily documents. Workspace curation begins with the cashier and storekeeper families — the recount after: two clicks.</p><p><b>Month three — the calendar starts.</b> The daily five-minute read begins (and catches, in its second week, a stuck email queue that module 6 will fix properly). The weekly user delta runs against HR's notes and finds two leavers still enabled — module 2's lifecycle procedure lands the same week, which is exactly where this track goes next.</p><p>The MD's note in the month-three file: <i>the platform now has an owner, a map, and a schedule. The modules were always run well; now the thing they run on is run well too.</i></p>"),
L("Chapter 9 — Common mistakes & the first law", "11", "<p>The recurring platform-custody failures, then the review list.</p><p><b>Pattern 1 — the unnamed function.</b> Symptom: no one owns the machinery; every symptom in this chapter present at once; the implementation partner as accidental, absent sysadmin. Cause: the role never named (Chapter 1). Fix: one named owner with a named backstop, the custody map taught, the calendar started — the function exists the day those three exist.</p><p><b>Pattern 2 — settings by drift.</b> Symptom: a System Settings page nobody can explain; formats and precision changed mid-life; session and password controls at install defaults years on. Cause: settings treated as toggles, changed per complaint, never recorded (Chapter 5). Fix: the recorded walk — every field a written decision — changes as governed events, and the annual re-walk as the only routine occasion.</p><p><b>Pattern 3 — the uncurated Desk.</b> Symptom: every user seeing every module; new staff trained by fear; misfiled documents in doctypes nobody should have opened; the owner never reading the system because the system shows too much. Cause: workspaces never curated, modules never trimmed (Chapter 6). Fix: the per-job-family curation procedure, the allowed-modules defaults, and the annual test-login audit with its click count.</p><p><b>Pattern 4 — the spreadsheet shadow system.</b> Symptom: exports everywhere; decisions made on snapshots days old; \"the report\" meaning a spreadsheet someone maintains by hand. Cause: the list/filter/report machinery never taught (Chapter 4). Fix: saved filters and Report Builder reports built with the askers, the live-versus-snapshot lesson taught, and export permission governed like the real permission it is.</p><p><b>Pattern 5 — the eloquent-symptom report.</b> Symptom: platform issues reported as \"it's not working\", escalated with heat and no facts; fixes slow because diagnosis starts from zero. Cause: no report discipline (Chapter 8). Fix: the five-item format taught and templated — versions, time, what changed, reproduction with the whole error text, scope — and the incident book receiving every case, local or raised.</p><p><b>The review list.</b> Before the quiz, confirm you can: state the custody map — site, apps, Desk, bench — and place any reported problem on it; use the awesome bar's three moves; read a doctype's grammar — fields, Link fields, child tables, naming — and the docstatus lifecycle with why nothing honest is deleted; build a filtered list, save it, flip it to report view, and name the three report kinds in escalation order; walk System Settings' blocks with the three rules; curate a workspace by the four-step procedure and defend the click-count audit; recite the calendar by frequency; and write a five-item support report.</p><p><b>The first law: the platform has a named owner — every setting is a written decision, and the machinery is governed like the books it carries.</b> Module 2 opens the largest subject in the sysadmin's custody: users, roles, and permissions.</p>"),
]

